    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d574">

                <pb facs="0430" n="426" sameAs="frus:frus1938v04/pg426"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">393.115 Standard Vacuum Oil Company/46:
                    Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The First Secretary of Embassy in China</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Salisbury</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-08-03T11:00:00+08:00">August 3, 1938—11
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received August 3—6 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>484. Reference paragraph 1 of Embassy’s 478, July 31, noon. Colonel Marston
                    states that he prefers that his opinion be expressed <pb facs="0431" n="427"
                        xml:id="pg427"/> as follows: he believes that guards should be furnished in
                    the interests of American trade, provided that there are no diplomatic
                    objections and provided that the guard can be placed on a separate convoying
                    tug.</p>
                <p>Sent to Ambassador [aboard] <hi rendition="#italic">Luzon</hi>.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Salisbury</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
